`serve --complete` on an `@rpc` key is refused before a session opens
(G-05c). RFC 05 §2.1's checklist row is "`complete` — **never** on an `@rpc`
queryable": zenoh's default query target (`BestMatching`) short-circuits to a
single queryable the moment any matching one is declared complete, so a mock
that claims completeness on a procedure key silently collapses every
default-target fleet call to its one reply. The dev tool refuses to model
the violation.

It exits **2** since #507: a refusal of the command line, where it used to
be a bare 1 that read "the act failed".

```console
$ zenctl serve v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/@rpc/sysinfo/processes {} --complete
? 2
Error: --complete on an @rpc key expression: RFC 05 §2.1 forbids it — a `complete` @rpc queryable short-circuits every BestMatching fleet call to this one responder, silently collapsing the fleet to a single reply. Serve the procedure without --complete.

```

The parameters half of a selector does not hide the plane chunk:

```console
$ zenctl serve v1/*/@rpc/sysinfo/processes?sort=cpu {} --complete
? 2
Error: --complete on an @rpc key expression: RFC 05 §2.1 forbids it — a `complete` @rpc queryable short-circuits every BestMatching fleet call to this one responder, silently collapsing the fleet to a single reply. Serve the procedure without --complete.

```

`--i-know` does not move it — the checklist row is a "never", and it is
asked before the acknowledgement is:

```console
$ zenctl serve v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/@rpc/sysinfo/processes {} --complete --i-know
? 2
Error: --complete on an @rpc key expression: RFC 05 §2.1 forbids it — a `complete` @rpc queryable short-circuits every BestMatching fleet call to this one responder, silently collapsing the fleet to a single reply. Serve the procedure without --complete.

```

## A mock that answers real GETs needs `--i-know` (#507)

`serve` stands a queryable up on a bus, and a queryable answers real queries.
On a wildcard it stands in front of every key it intersects, across every
producer's — `serve 'prod/v1/**' x` answers production applications' GETs
for state with one static body. `--complete` on any key makes zenoh's default
(`BestMatching`) target stop at this responder, so a read of that state gets
the mock's body instead of the storage's. Either is a decision about a bus,
and both now take the verb's one acknowledgement; both are refused before a
session opens.

```console
$ zenctl serve 'v1/**' {}
? 2
Error: v1/** is a wildcard: this queryable answers every GET it intersects, across every producer's keys, with one static body — state a storage would have answered included. Pass --i-know to mean it.

```

```console
$ zenctl serve v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/state/demo/health {} --complete
? 2
Error: --complete claims this responder holds all the data v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/state/demo/health names: zenoh's default (BestMatching) GET stops at a complete queryable, so a production read of that state gets this static body instead of the storage's. Pass --i-know to mean it.

```

With it, the mock goes up (here against the dead-bus fixture, so the session
is where it stops):

```console
$ zenctl serve 'v1/**' {} --i-know --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 1
Error: failed to open the Zenoh session

Caused by:
    Unable to connect to any of [Single(tcp/127.0.0.1:1)]!

```
